75]},"0c7d5546c53b0e66c03d":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"supporting details","bbox":[205.1300048828125,458.09002685546875,302.58599853515625,471.4700012207031]},"c0558d93e17d91f676c3":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"paragraph","bbox":[349.1499938964844,458.09002685546875,407.1860046386719,471.4700012207031]},"a036a985f13b742faa50":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"__________1.The most important piece of information the author wants you to know","bbox":[72.0,499.6900329589844,526.5260009765625,513.0700073242188]},"ec748630455083d4cd6d":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"about the concept of a material","bbox":[148.52000427246094,513.4900512695312,316.58599853515625,526.8700561523438]},"87595f10590e4859ab1d":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"__________2. Facts, statements, examples-specifics which guide us to a full understanding of the main idea","bbox":[72.0,541.0900268554688,492.906005859375,568.27001953125]},"dd9ac37e7da5c88f6d96":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"__________3. A self-contained unit of a discourse in writing dealing with a particular point or idea","bbox":[72.0,582.510009765625,529.0059814453125,609.6900634765625]},"eb29f6ae5bf86ce99f9a":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"__________4. The type of the main idea that is not directly stated in the text","bbox":[72.0,623.9100341796875,479.1059875488281,637.2900390625]},"6353e22f201742ec54d5":{"type":"paragraph","page":3,"text":"__________5. The type of the main idea that is directly stated in the text","bbox":[72.0,651.4100341796875,459.1059875488281,664.7900390625]},"be3ac54ea752de598fcb":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"Agreeing or disagreeing with a material viewed shows our stand on a particular","bbox":[108.0199966430664,134.75999450683594,526.423828125,148.13999938964844]},"dc654bd503e90ba10bac":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"issue. It indicates whether we have the same (agreement) or a different idea (disagreement) as the one presented. There are expressions we can use to show either agreement or disagreement, such as:","bbox":[72.0,149.66001892089844,526.3900756835938,215.7400360107422]},"a0b00ff47dc60cb7031c":{"type":"table","rows":[["AGREEMENT","DISAGREEMENT"],[" I agree with you 100 percent.  I couldn't agree with you more.  That's so true.  That's for sure.  (slang) Tell me about it!  You're absolutely right.  Absolutely.  That's exactly how I feel.  Exactly.  I'm afraid I agree with James.  I have to side with Dad on this one.  No doubt about it.  (agree with the negative statement) Me neither.  (weak) I suppose so. /I guess so.  You have a point there.  I was just going to say that."," I don't think so.  (strong) No way.  I'm afraid I disagree.  (strong) I totally disagree.  I beg to differ.  (strong) I'd say the exact opposite.  Not necessarily.  That's not always true.  That's not always the case.  No, I'm not so sure about that."]],"bbox":[72.16666666666667,225.28666178385416,523.2133382161459,541.7833455403646],"text":"AGREEMENT DISAGREEMENT  I agree with you 100 percent.  I couldn't agree with you more.  That's so true.  That's for sure.  (slang) Tell me about it!  You're absolutely right.  Absolutely.  That's exactly how I feel.  Exactly.  I'm afraid I agree with James.  I have to side with Dad on this one.  No doubt about it.  (agree with the negative statement) Me neither.  (weak) I suppose so. /I guess so.  You have a point there.  I was just going to say that.  I don't think so.  (strong) No way.  I'm afraid I disagree.  (strong) I totally disagree.  I beg to differ.  (strong) I'd say the exact opposite.  Not necessarily.  That's not always true.  That's not always the case.  No, I'm not so sure about that.","page":4},"d65605b0116943933479":{"type":"figure","page":4,"bbox":[128.89999389648438,251.5,466.3999938964844,589.0],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/9c724351983a6b3fb728c2f9.webp","width":300,"height":300,"monochrome":false},"85a3d3acd7b32ade49b3":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"Moreover, there are situations when stating your reasons for agreeing or","bbox":[108.0199966430664,564.9900512695312,526.6879272460938,578.3700561523438]},"693514a93468d77e75ec":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"disagreeing is necessary. It requires that you carefully consider arguments/evidence for and against the particular position or viewpoint raised in the material. Here are guide questions you can use.","bbox":[72.0,579.9100341796875,526.6640014648438,623.0900268554688]},"bc3a069427a7655aa13e":{"type":"paragraph","page":4,"text":"1. What are the strengths/advantages and weaknesses/disadvantages of _____________? 2. What evidence supports and contradicts ________________? 3. What are arguments for and counterarguments against ______________?","bbox":[108.0199966430664,632.6100463867188,526.471923828125,687.3900146484375]},"795dada49247953cd1e6":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Directions: Watch the video, then answer the questions that follow.","bbox":[72.0,119.86003112792969,433.8160095214844,133.2400360107422]},"9fecf8dda2295bd4e96a":{"type":"figure","page":5,"bbox":[73.30000305175781,138.00001525878906,523.2999877929688,347.20001220703125],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/f36d5fefd4050d957d452365.webp","width":1366,"height":768,"monochrome":true,"transcript":"Importance\nVACCINES\nENG"},"3f1853498e035ac4cfb4":{"type":"figure","page":5,"bbox":[128.89999389648438,251.5,466.3999938964844,589.0],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/9c724351983a6b3fb728c2f9.webp","width":300,"height":300,"monochrome":false},"20eb3bb921e68424c0a0":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Reference: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kNw8V_Fkw28&t=9s","bbox":[72.0,355.6950378417969,428.4380187988281,367.96002197265625]},"0767954ed359f89e7bc5":{"type":"paragraph","page":5,"text":"Video transcript: Let's learn about the importance of vaccination. While a vast majority of parents get their children vaccinated, very few know how they work and some are skeptical. Over the past 50 years, immunization has saved millions of lives and made once common diseases like measles, rubella, tetanus, hepatitis B, mumps, and pertussis. Vaccination protects you and your loved ones from serious illnesses and complications from those illnesses such as hearing loss, seizures, brain damage, and death. Not vaccinating your kids puts them at risk for all of these things. But it also puts countless other people at risk as well. If your child isn't immune, they are capable of spreading the disease to other people who have weak immune systems, aren't vaccinated, or are too young to be vaccinated. Contracting one of these diseases that only has the potential to kill those who can't be vaccinated infants and people who are immunocompromised but could also cause an outbreak an outbreak is a cluster of cases of a particular disease within a community. Outbreaks and epidemics caused by unvaccinated individuals are becoming more and more common. For example, one person with measles who didn't yet know they were sick. Disney's Magic Kingdom recently started an outbreak that spread across half of the United States and infected 189 people. There was no magic that caused this disease to spread so fast and so wide. It happened because people were unvaccinated, and measles is extremely contagious. Measles has been declared eliminated in the U.S in 2000, but now it's back. To keep deadly diseases from returning and spreading, we need to have enough people vaccinated to act like a force field. Those that are immune surround an infected individual and prevent disease in taking hold in a community. It is everyone's responsibility to prevent measles and other diseases from turning the happiest place on earth into the home base of a dangerous upgrade. Vaccines are safe and effective. They may not be magic, but they'll protect you and your loved ones. We are a community. We vaccinate to protect. Hashtag immunity in your community.","bbox":[80.4000015258789,379.2425231933594,517.5675659179688,748.7899780273438]},"016e79d450117ad2b764":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"A. Directions: Use one of the given expressions to agree or disagree with each statement. 1. Vaccination is essential to protect people against contagious diseases.","bbox":[72.0,122.06004333496094,526.47802734375,173.2400360107422]},"1145b701a5c7dad94630":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"2. Vaccines work like magic. They wipe off diseases in a snap.","bbox":[90.0250015258789,215.06004333496094,429.6059875488281,228.44004821777344]},"aeda4e9022b00f75a980":{"type":"figure","page":6,"bbox":[128.89999389648438,251.5,466.3999938964844,589.0],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/9c724351983a6b3fb728c2f9.webp","width":300,"height":300,"monochrome":false},"cfde9b9947fe93d7fe1b":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"3. Measles and rubella are diseases that vaccines cannot cure.","bbox":[90.0250015258789,270.260009765625,432.9159851074219,283.6399841308594]},"8f5f1858af5bae038bd9":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"4. Unvaccinated individuals can cause outbreaks and pandemics.","bbox":[90.0250015258789,339.2600402832031,445.6059875488281,352.6400146484375]},"0eaa138162700f0099ea":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"5. Community members need to support vaccination to stop the spread of diseases.","bbox":[90.0250015258789,394.49005126953125,526.68798828125,421.6700134277344]},"31566379d35a364d77fc":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"B. Directions: Answer the following questions. 1. What is the video about? _____________________________________________","bbox":[72.0,472.49005126953125,524.7160034179688,509.050048828125]},"4851a4ed98da9fb9c821":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"2. What point was raised in it? __________________________________________","bbox":[72.0,522.7460327148438,522.6160278320312,536.7500610351562]},"3c83c81e0a3b28d6dc64":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"3. What were arguments for or against such a point provided?","bbox":[72.0,564.14599609375,403.6860046386719,578.1500244140625]},"130d6df360e30a9dc05d":{"type":"paragraph","page":6,"text":"4. Do you agree with the point raised? Why or why not?","bbox":[72.0,633.2659912109375,373.7860107421875,647.27001953125]},"fdf95bf7518278cdfab5":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":"Agreeing or disagreeing with a material viewed shows our stand on a particular","bbox":[108.0199966430664,140.56004333496094,526.423828125,153.94004821777344]},"a0009381ed356bc57e16":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":"issue. It indicates whether we have the same (agreement) or a different idea (disagreement) with the one presented in it.","bbox":[72.0,155.4600067138672,526.27197265625,183.7400360107422]},"eed75af2ac2c0ca31c17":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":"The following are expressions to show either agreement or disagreement:","bbox":[108.0199966430664,193.25999450683594,502.21600341796875,206.63999938964844]},"c73c92b0a35d227d5d33":{"type":"heading","page":7,"text":"Agreement","bbox":[143.22999572753906,237.25999450683594,209.2659912109375,250.63999938964844]},"75da7776ba2e9cf67cf5":{"type":"heading","page":7,"text":"Disagreement","bbox":[331.6499938964844,240.36500549316406,407.5080261230469,252.6300048828125]},"63063f82d968d0c8aa74":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":" I agree with you, 100","bbox":[97.625,251.87005615234375,230.35198974609375,266.5220642089844]},"91245760dfb295bed9c8":{"type":"figure","page":7,"bbox":[128.89999389648438,251.5,466.3999938964844,589.0],"src":"/assets/curriculum/figures/9c724351983a6b3fb728c2f9.webp","width":300,"height":300,"monochrome":false},"87259eec0e6785d960cd":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":" I don't think so.","bbox":[313.6499938964844,263.6700439453125,415.2860107421875,276.2400207519531]},"703f43313bcdd2438d8f":{"type":"paragraph","page":7,"text":"percent.  I couldn't agree with you","bbox":[115.62000274658203,266.760009765625,247.86001586914062,294.9120178222656]},"4056f6eea34d2cd92087":{"type":"paragraph","pag